WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.369
Ukrainian officials dismissing a claim

00:00:02.369 --> 00:00:03.903
by the head of the Wagner

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:06.139
Private Army Group, who had said that

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:07.507
Russian forces

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:11.044
hold back mode in, quote, legal terms.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:12.645
Yevgeny Prigozhin

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.881
made that claim in this video,

00:00:15.181 --> 00:00:16.483
which he said showed him

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:17.717
raising a Russian flag

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:19.686
at the side of a council building,

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:20.620
a Ukraine

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:21.121
military

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:22.522
spokesman says the video

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:24.557
is incomprehensible.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.558
David McKenzie

00:00:25.558 --> 00:00:27.761
joining us with the very latest.

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:28.428
He's on the ground

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:30.530
in Kiev in Ukraine for you David.

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:33.800
Well, that's right.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:34.334
That video,

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.736
which is, of course, taken at night

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.438
and not exactly

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:40.073
clear what we are seeing there.

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:41.241
But Prigozhin claimed

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:43.309
that they had in a legal sense,

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:44.044
taken back

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.046
that heavily contested city

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.947
in the eastern part of the front.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:49.082
Now, Ukrainian officials

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:51.151
saying that's, quote, wishful thinking.

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:51.951
They say

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.354
while that is a very difficult situation,

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:55.855
they still control certain

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.290
sections of that city.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.124
And importantly,

00:00:58.124 --> 00:00:59.059
they say they are able

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:02.395
to get food, medicine and the wounded

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:05.565
out of that area, as well as ammunition.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.801
So and they also point out,

00:01:07.801 --> 00:01:09.836
I think, which is important, that over

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:11.371
many, many months

00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:12.972
the Russians and the private

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:13.907
military contractors

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:15.608
have not been able to take back money.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.777
That much is clear.

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:20.880
You know, the ongoing shelling

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:22.082
and rocket attacks

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:23.049
and missile attacks

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:24.284
in the eastern part of the country

00:01:24.284 --> 00:01:26.219
and frankly, throughout this country

00:01:26.219 --> 00:01:28.254
have had a devastating toll

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:30.423
on soldiers and civilians alike.

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:34.561
We got rare access inside a team of U.S.

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:35.161
and Canadian

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:35.995
surgeons working to

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:38.264
reconstruct Ukrainian faces.

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.566
I must warn some viewers

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:40.667
they could find some of

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:42.001
these images, disturbing

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:44.137
levels

00:01:45.004 --> 00:01:45.705
and. Right.

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:46.306
The impact

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.541
of war is hard to look at my finger.

00:01:49.109 --> 00:01:51.811
The difficulty that that I'm having

00:01:51.811 --> 00:01:52.312
is that

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:54.314
I don't know what anything looks like

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.282
behind the skin here.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.550
I can make an opening

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.018
that looks like there's an eye,

00:01:59.018 --> 00:01:59.886
but they're never going

00:01:59.886 --> 00:02:01.621
to look like normal eyelids.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:02.989
And the surgical realities

00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.992
are nothing like civilian life.

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:06.726
Okay.

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.629
How do you compare it to here in Ukraine?

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:11.064
Well, the level of complexity

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:13.900
for these cases is significantly

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:15.201
more elaborate

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.237
and significantly more complex.

00:02:18.004 --> 00:02:19.739
We think we can get the mouth

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:20.607
working better.

00:02:20.607 --> 00:02:21.674
Face a future mission.

00:02:21.674 --> 00:02:23.143
Director Anthony Perseid

00:02:23.143 --> 00:02:24.744
says the blast injuries

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.246
are often devastating.

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:28.348
One of the things that we can do

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:29.282
is improve

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:30.783
the appearance of the scar,

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:31.818
multi-level bone

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.553
and soft tissue injuries.

00:02:33.553 --> 00:02:36.756
It really does not get any more complex

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.559
than this, even in a combat scenario.

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:40.627
They brought together

00:02:40.627 --> 00:02:42.128
highly specialized plastic

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:43.796
surgeons, anesthesiologists

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:45.832
and nurses from the US and Canada

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:48.034
to reconstruct and repair.

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:49.636
All that bone is missing

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:50.970
what many cannot.

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.505
And if we can do that,

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:53.706
then that certainly is

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:55.275
a fulfilling opportunity.

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:57.310
So it's not just a physical change,

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.847
it's a psychological help you hope.

00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:03.116
Absolutely. Absolutely.

00:03:03.216 --> 00:03:05.151
And it's a psychological help

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:06.753
not just for the patient,

00:03:06.753 --> 00:03:08.354
but also for their family.

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:11.558
Roman Polanski is one of their patients.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.195
He's invited us to his home

00:03:17.096 --> 00:03:19.299
What do I think of him?

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.035
I'm proud of my son,

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:24.304
says his mother, Lisa Thomas.

00:03:24.304 --> 00:03:25.038
I'm proud of him.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.039
I'm proud of the fact

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:27.440
that he didn't run away.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:28.408
He didn't hide.

00:03:28.408 --> 00:03:30.310
I should call some

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:31.978
early in the war.

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:33.213
His mechanized infantry

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.747
brigade faced the brunt

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:36.182
of Russia's invasion

00:03:36.182 --> 00:03:37.884
and their oncoming tanks.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:40.019
Are you surprised that you survived

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.022
I do not understand how I survived,

00:03:43.022 --> 00:03:44.958
he says. Doesn't that make it worse?

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:46.025
I don't even understand

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:47.594
how I got through the shelling

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:49.562
because it was dark.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:51.464
The night I was hanging out,

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:52.699
I was concussed.

00:03:52.699 --> 00:03:54.601
My whole face was covered in blood

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.805
Shrapnel went right through me.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:03:59.606
It shouldn't lose.

00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:01.207
As he says, many in

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:02.942
his brigade were lost.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:06.079
We were all like one family, he says.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.616
You know, somewhere you feel your guilt.

00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:11.718
But I didn't also die like they did.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:15.855
Roman lived 

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:22.128
And this will be his third surgery

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:23.596
with Dr. John Croteau.

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:25.832
What what bothers you the most now?

00:04:26.199 --> 00:04:28.334
Our hope is that at some point

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:29.902
they leave happy

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.704
and then I don't see them again.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:32.805
On my end,

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:35.008
I have to appreciate we're making steps

00:04:35.475 --> 00:04:37.777
because we don't fix them

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.412
if we make them better.

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:40.980
So this is where we shine,

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.615
which is in the operating room.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.250
All of the steps and activities

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:44.884
that we were doing

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:46.386
before getting here

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:48.454
is really to get us to this point.

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:49.622
Roman surgery

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:51.591
is one of the first of the day.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:52.292
He says Dr.

00:04:52.292 --> 00:04:53.893
Friedel and the team have already

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:56.462
put him back together and saved his life.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:57.930
Dr.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:58.531
Frodo is

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:00.300
working to remove a cheek implant

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.368
just a tiny bit higher honor.

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:04.737
Among the margins in this kind of surgery

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:05.838
are very small,

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:07.940
but the differences for the patients

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:09.242
can be huge.

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:11.010
A person's appearance

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:13.413
is a reflection of their inner spirit,

00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.481
of their inner self to the world

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:17.183
and we must never forget that.

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:18.451
But everyone wants to have

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:19.819
a facial appearance

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.721
that others want to look at.

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:23.122
I would want to get to know him.

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:26.225
It's part of the human condition

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:28.227
I've been in touch with Dr.

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:30.530
Frodo, Becky, over the next few days

00:05:30.530 --> 00:05:32.665
after that surgery of Ramon.

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:34.634
He says that Roman's doing well.

00:05:34.634 --> 00:05:36.469
The swelling will eventually come down.

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:37.470
They're coming back in

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.205
just a matter of weeks.

00:05:39.205 --> 00:05:40.373
As we say in that report,

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:42.709
there takes a long term commitment.

00:05:42.709 --> 00:05:44.110
And part of the commitment

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.813
is the patients themselves deciding

00:05:46.813 --> 00:05:47.980
whether they want to go on

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.116
with these multiple surgeries

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:53.086
that at times make marginal differences.

00:05:53.086 --> 00:05:54.354
But in general,

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:55.755
they're making a huge difference

00:05:55.755 --> 00:05:57.657
in their lives. Becky.

00:05:57.657 --> 00:05:59.726
It is it is absolutely remarkable.

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:01.794
Their work is to be absolutely applauded.

